+# Rejection: arXiv 2504.18390
+
+Paper: [*Point-transitive and 1-rotational unitals of order 5*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.18390), Ivan Hetman, Taras Banakh, and Alex Ravsky (2025).
+
+## Decision
+
+No generator is shipped. The paper supports cheap exact verification of its listed designs, but it does not support an unlimited, scalable, hard witness family. In the terminology of the task, **V passes, while scalable G/G7 and H fail**. H fails on both Track A and Track B, so changing the track does not rescue the family.
+
+## What the full paper actually contains
+
+Section 1 fixes an order-5 unital as a Steiner system `S(2,6,126)`. It considers either an effective transitive action of a group of order 126, or an action of a group of order 125 with one fixed point. It also says that blocks are developed by left multiplication using a Cayley table.
+
+Sections 2 and 3 are catalogues. Each catalogue entry is a precomputed hyperbolic-frequency fingerprint followed by a completed difference family. The source has no theorem, definition, or proof environment in use and gives no search algorithm, complexity bound, parameterized regime, or running-time measurement. Section 3 says only that a computer calculation distinguished two pairs of equal-fingerprint designs. The order, point count, and block size never vary.
+
+The summary table describes 1,311 non-isomorphic designs across the named groups; 1,239 completed catalogue entries appear in this paper, with the remaining cyclic examples referred to earlier papers. This is a finite classification/data paper, not a construction theorem with a growing parameter.
+
+## STEP 0: what produces the certificate?
+
+The certificate producer is **table lookup**. For example, the first entry of Example 2.1 explicitly prints these five base blocks for `Z_125` (with `125` used below for infinity):
+
+```text
+[0,1,3,15,47,74]
+[0,4,9,20,65,103]
+[0,6,40,88,95,112]
+[0,8,18,41,76,104]
+[0,25,50,75,100,infinity]
+```
+
+I expanded this entry exactly under translation. The 625 raw translates deduplicate to 525 blocks. Their 7,875 unordered point-pairs are all distinct, which equals `C(126,2)`, so every pair occurs exactly once. A checker therefore needs only integer group operations, set normalization, and pair counting. This establishes V.
+
+The measured operation counts for that natural certificate are:
+
+| route | cost at the paper's only size |
+|---|---:|
+| Produce the compressed certificate by the paper's method | copy 5 displayed blocks = 30 point symbols; `Theta(1)` because order 5 is fixed |
+| Compact/no-tool route | the same 30-symbol lookup; or 30 label substitutions if a relabelling is supplied |
+| Naively develop all displayed base blocks | 3,625 modular additions over 625 raw translates |
+| Exact verification after deduplication | 525 blocks and 7,875 pair insertions |
+| Write the expanded design instead | 3,150 atomic point entries, exceeding the 256-element G9(c) cap |
+
+Thus the mechanical certificate-production cost and the putative compact route are the same: 30 copied/substituted symbols, a ratio of 1. There is no hidden invariant or change of variables compressing a large computation. The larger numbers belong only to checking or expanding a certificate already printed by the paper; they cannot be used as a hardness claim under the witness rule.
+
+## Why Track A fails
+
+There is no hardness theorem or hard parameter regime in Sections 1--3. More fundamentally, the native problem has the single fixed size `S(2,6,126)`, and the paper supplies the answers as an explicit finite table. A lookup in a fixed catalogue is constant-time with respect to any proposed `n`. Relabelling a listed design can generate many encodings, but a correct `canonical_key` must identify those encodings as the same problem.
+
+Consequently there is no honest Track A `hardness_basis`, no growing native `n`, and no way to satisfy G7. Worst-case hardness of an externally introduced exact-cover or design-completion problem would say nothing about a distribution made from this fixed table.
+
+## Why Track B also fails
+
+Track B requires a large mechanical route and a substantially shorter structural route. Here the only paper-backed short route is the explicit catalogue itself. Mechanical lookup and compact lookup both cost 30 symbol accesses for Example 2.1. If the catalogue is withheld from the solver, reconstructing an entry has no compact procedure in the paper; if it is supplied, the task is transcription.
+
+Randomly hiding listed blocks among decoys, asking for an undisclosed relabelling, or converting the blocks to exact cover could enlarge a search space, but those are benchmark inventions rather than results or transformations analyzed in this paper. They would manufacture the hardness in the wrapper. Supplying the relabelling makes transport a 30-lookup exercise; hiding it creates a group/design-isomorphism puzzle with no paper-backed compact route. Neither produces the Track B compression gap.
+
+The fingerprint column does not help. The paper explains a large direct enumeration of quadruples but supplies no sub-300-operation identity for deriving a fingerprint. Asking for it would test raw computation (or catalogue recall) and fail G9(c)'s intended-route cap.
+
+## Gate-level conclusion
+
+| requirement | result | reason |
+|---|---|---|
+| G, one certified instance | pass | A displayed difference family can be transported through a known relabelling. |
+| G, unlimited scalable family / G7 | **fail** | Native objects are fixed at 126 points; relabellings canonically collapse, and no growing construction is proved. |
+| H, Track A | **fail** | No hardness theorem or distributional regime; answers form an explicit finite catalogue. |
+| H, Track B | **fail** | Mechanical and compact certificate routes are both the same 30-entry lookup/transport. |
+| V | pass | Develop group orbits and count all 7,875 pairs exactly. |
+| G9(c), compressed base blocks | pass | 30 atoms, but this form fails H. |
+| G9(c), full incidence system | **fail** | 3,150 atoms exceed the 256-atom output cap. |
+
+The prior triage proposal--constructing a block incidence system from a prescribed automorphism-group action--is valid as a verifier and as a way to replay a catalogue entry. It is not an unlimited hard problem generator. Generalizing to other orders or adding a completion/decoy layer would require a theorem or distribution absent from arXiv:2504.18390, so no `gen_2504_18390.py` was written.

